What if the Load Balancer Itself Goes Down? 🚨

We design highly available systems on AWS — multiple EC2 instances, Auto Scaling, RDS Multi-AZ, S3 durability, health checks, monitoring dashboards…
But here’s the uncomfortable question:

👉 What if the Load Balancer itself goes down?

It’s the single entry point to your application.
If it fails (misconfiguration, DNS issue, AZ outage, security group mistake), your entire application becomes unreachable.

Suddenly:
EC2 instances are healthy… but no traffic 🚫
RDS is running… but no connections 🚫
Auto Scaling is scaling… but no users 🚫

CloudWatch alarms are screaming 🔔
And users?

They only see: “Site can’t be reached.”

💡 Key reminders for architects & DevOps teams:
Deploy ALB/NLB across multiple AZs
Configure proper health checks
Use Route 53 health checks + failover routing
Avoid hidden single points of failure
Test chaos scenarios (GameDays are gold!)
Consider multi-region for business-critical apps

High availability isn’t just about scaling 
compute.

It’s about protecting the front door of your application.
